</ul>

<h2 class="mt-8">Requirements</h2>
<ul class="l-ui-list mt-4">
  <li>Ruby on Rails >= 8.0</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/layered-ai-public/layered-ui-rails">layered-ui-rails</a> ~> 0.14</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/activerecord-hackery/ransack">Ransack</a> ~> 4.0</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/ddnexus/pagy">Pagy</a> ~> 43.2</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="mt-8">Agent skill</h2>
<p class="mt-4">
  An <a href="https://agentskills.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">agent skill</a> is included so AI coding agents can work with <code>layered-resource-rails</code> in your project. Once installed, the agent can handle the full setup - just ask it to add <code>layered-resource-rails</code> to your app and it will install the gem, scaffold resources, and wire up routes.
</p>
<p class="mt-4"><strong>Project install</strong> - scoped to a single repo, available to all contributors:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bin/rails generate layered:resource:install_agent_skill</code></pre>
<p class="mt-4"><strong>Global install</strong> - available across all your projects:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>./install-skill.sh</code></pre>
<p class="mt-4">Or install remotely without cloning the repo:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/layered-ai-public/layered-resource-rails/main/install-skill.sh | sh</code></pre>

<h2 class="mt-8">Installation</h2>
<p class="mt-4">Add to your Gemfile:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>gem "layered-resource-rails"</code></pre>
<p class="l-ui-mt-4">Then run:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>bundle install</code></pre>

<h2 class="mt-8">Quick start</h2>
<p class="mt-4">The fastest path - scaffold the model, migration, resource, and route in one shot:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>rails g layered:resource:scaffold post title:string body:text</code></pre>
<p class="mt-4">This invokes Rails' built-in <code>model</code> generator, writes <code>app/layered_resources/post_resource.rb</code> with <code>columns</code> and <code>fields</code> derived from the attributes, and appends <code>layered_resources :posts</code> to <code>config/routes.rb</code>. Views are intentionally not generated - the gem's defaults render until you eject them with <code>rails g layered:resource:views posts</code>.</p>
<p class="mt-4">Pass <code>--skip-model</code> if the model already exists. Restrict which CRUD actions get routed with <code>--actions index show</code> (emits <code>only:</code>) or <code>--except destroy</code> (emits <code>except:</code>). Pass <code>--controller</code> to also eject a controller and wire it into the route, or <code>--views</code> to eject the templates upfront.</p>
<p class="mt-4">If the model already exists and you just want the resource class plus its route, use <code>rails g layered:resource post title:string body:text</code> instead. It writes <code>app/layered_resources/post_resource.rb</code> and appends <code>layered_resources :posts</code> - pass <code>--skip-route</code> to skip the route line.</p>

<h2 class="mt-8">Setup</h2>

<h3 class="l-ui-mt-4">1. Routes</h3>
<p class="mt-4">Use <code>layered_resources</code> in your routes to register a model for CRUD management:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code># config/routes.rb
layered_resources :posts</code></pre>
<p class="mt-4">Restrict actions with <code>only:</code> or <code>except:</code>:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>layered_resources :posts, only: [:index]             # read-only
layered_resources :posts, only: %i[index destroy]    # list + delete
layered_resources :posts, except: [:destroy]         # everything but delete</code></pre>

<h3 class="l-ui-mt-4">2. Resource definition</h3>
<p class="mt-4">Create a resource class in <code>app/layered_resources/</code> that inherits from <code>Layered::Resource::Base</code>:</p>
<pre class="l-ui-surface mt-4"><code>class PostResource < Layered::Resource::Base
  model Post

  columns [
    { attribute: :title, primary: true },
    { attribute: :created_at, label: "Created" }
  ]

  # Fields with a presence validation on the model are automatically marked as required.
  fields [
    { attribute: :title },
    { attribute: :body, as: :text }
  ]

  search_fields [:title, :body]
end</code></pre>

<h3 class="l-ui-mt-4">3. Resource configuration</h3>
<p class="mt-4">Override these class methods on your resource to customise behaviour:</p>
<% config_methods = [
  { method: "columns", purpose: "Columns displayed on the index table" },
  { method: "fields", purpose: "Form fields for new/edit (empty = read-only, no CRUD forms)" },
  { method: "search_fields", purpose: "Ransack search attributes" },
  { method: "default_sort", purpose: "Default sort order for the index" },
  { method: "per_page", purpose: "Pagination size (default 15)" },
  { method: "scope(controller)", purpose: "Base scope (override for tenant isolation)" },
  { method: "build_record(controller)", purpose: "How to instantiate new records" },
  { method: "after_save_path(controller, record)", purpose: "Redirect target after create/update/destroy" }
] %>

<div class="mt-4">
  <%= l_ui_table(config_methods,
        columns: [
          { attribute: :method, label: "Method", render: ->(r) { tag.code(r[:method]) } },
          { attribute: :purpose, label: "Purpose", render: ->(r) { r[:purpose] } }
        ],
        caption: "Resource configuration methods") %>
</div>

<h2 class="mt-8">Authentication</h2>
<p class="mt-4">
  <code>Layered::Resource::ResourcesController</code> inherits from your app's <code>ApplicationController</code>, so any <code>before_action</code> declared there (e.g. Devise's <code>authenticate_user!</code>) already protects every layered resource request.
</p>

